.TH "UBERTOOTH\-DUMP" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-dump\fR \- output a continuous stream of received bits
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-dump [\-b] [\-c | \-l | \-g] [\-d <filename\.bin>|\-] [\-U <index|serial>]
ubertooth\-dump [\-U <index|serial>] \-V
ubertooth\-dump \-h
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
This tool dumps the raw bitstream received from the CC2400 radio to STDOUT or a file (if specified with \fB\-d\fR)\. It can configure the radio to demodulate Classic Bluetooth with \fB\-c\fR (default), or Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) with \fB\-l\fR\. Optionally it can decode the binary data and print ASCII 0 and 1 using \fB\-b\fR\.
.P
\fB\-g\fR is experimental: see THE GENERIC RECEIVE below for what it asks of a configuration, and expect the option and its output to change\.
.P
\fB\-g\fR runs the board's generic receive instead\. That mode programs no demodulator of its own: it receives with whatever CC2400 configuration is already in the radio, which \fBubertooth\-debug \-w\fR is how you put there\. It is what the board has for a 2\.4 GHz protocol that is not Bluetooth, and ubertooth(7), GENERIC RADIO MODE is the worked example\.
.P
The dump is one record per block of symbols the board sends\. Without \fB\-b\fR a record is 68 bytes: the host time the block arrived, as Unix epoch seconds in 4 bytes big\-endian, then the whole 64\-byte USB receive packet, 50 bytes of which are the symbols packed 8 per byte\. With \fB\-b\fR a record is one 401\-byte line: the block's 400 symbols as ASCII \fB0\fR and \fB1\fR, then a newline\. The records are back to back with nothing between them, so a reader counts from the start of the dump\. A keep\-alive, which a quiet capture sends and which carries no symbols, writes no record either way\.
.P
The capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-b\fR
Print ASCII 0 and 1 instead of raw data
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
Classic Bluetooth modulation (default)
.TP
\fB\-l\fR
Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) modulation
.TP
\fB\-g\fR
Generic receive (experimental): demodulate with the CC2400 configuration already in the radio rather than programming one\. Cannot be given with \fB\-c\fR or \fB\-l\fR
.TP
\fB\-d <filename\.bin>\fR
Dump to file instead of stdout\. A lone \fB\-\fR is standard output, where the stream goes anyway without \fB\-d\fR
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-dump\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
The board demodulates one modulation at a time, so \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-l\fR in one command are refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: \-l cannot be combined with \-c; a capture uses one modulation\fR\. The second one used to be the one used, and the other was dropped without a word\.
.P
\fB\-g\fR and a modulation ask for two different things, so they are refused together as well: \fBerror: \-g cannot be combined with \-c; the generic receive uses the modulation already in the radio\fR\. \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-l\fR name a demodulator for the board to program, and \fB\-g\fR says to use the one already configured, so a dump that took both would overwrite part of the configuration it was asked to read with\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-d\fR names its file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\. The board is opened and its firmware is checked before that, so a run refused for either reason leaves no empty file behind either\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Without \fB\-d\fR, standard output carries the symbol stream and nothing else, and so does \fB\-d \-\fR\. Errors, warnings and the per\-block timestamp go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: /no/such/dir/capture\.bin: No such file or directory\fR\. That line comes after the board has been selected and its firmware accepted, because that is when the file is opened\.
.P
A terminal is refused either way, before any device is opened: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write binary dump data to it\fR, exit 1\. A dump is written for a file or another program, not for a screen\. \fB\-b\fR is refused too, and the line names what it writes: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write the bitstream dump to it\fR\. That stream is text rather than binary, and no more use on a screen at tens of thousands of characters a second\. Redirect or pipe standard output instead, or give \fB\-d\fR a file\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-d' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\.
.SH "GAPS IN A CAPTURE"
A dump that is losing records says so\. The board holds captured records in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next one\. The first time it happens the dump warns \fBwarning: the recording board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the dump ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost records: one full queue can swallow several, and the board sends no number\. Nothing in the dump marks where a gap is, so a dump with gaps is continuous only between them\.
.P
A capture transfer error is reported the same way\. The chip's transfer controller can report an error on the channel the board receives into: the record it was filling had arrived, so it is kept and marked, while some of its bytes may be wrong and whatever the fault cut short before it is lost\. The first mark warns \fBwarning: the recording board had a transfer error while capturing; what it was receiving was kept, but some of those bytes may be wrong and the capture may be missing packets around it\fR, and when the dump ends it says how many there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 transfer errors where a packet may be wrong or missing\fR\. ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-w\fR reports the board's own count of them since power\-on, over every capture the board has run\.
.P
Symbols the board wrote over are reported the same way\. The board receives into one block of symbols while its capture loop reads the one before it, and when a block finishes arriving before the loop has read that one, the new symbols go over the old ones\. The block is gone before anything looked in it for a packet, so this loss has no packet to point at and nothing in the output marks where it happened\. The first one warns \fBwarning: the recording board fell behind while capturing and wrote new symbols over a block it had not read yet; whatever was in the air then is missing from this capture\fR, and when the dump ends it says how many such places there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 places where the board wrote new symbols over a block it had not read\fR\. The count is of those places, not of the blocks lost at them: the board has one flag for this and no field to send a number in, so one place can stand for several blocks\.
.P
A dump whose receiver is being driven harder than it can measure says so, once: \fBwarning: the signal is at the top of the receiver's range, so the board cannot measure it and may not demodulate it; the boards may be too close together, or the transmit power too high\fR\. The radio reports the power of each block of air on a scale that runs out, and a receiver held at the top of it is also past where it demodulates reliably, so the symbols in the dump are not to be trusted\. Put more distance or more attenuation between the boards, or turn the transmit power down\. It takes a receiver held at the top of the scale: one loud packet is not reported\. When the dump ends it says how many such stretches there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 stretches where the signal was at the top of the receiver's range\fR\.
.P
With \fB\-c\fR or \fB\-l\fR, which is every run that does not ask for \fB\-g\fR, the board sends a record for every block of air, so the warning arrives whether or not anything is decoding\. With \fB\-g\fR it depends on the configuration in the radio, as the receive itself does: left with GRMDM's packet\-mode bit clear the board reads a stream of symbols and reports this as \fB\-c\fR does, while with the bit set it waits on a sync word and takes no readings at all, so those records state no signal figure and nothing is reported however loud the air\. See ubertooth(7)\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after the dump is interrupted (Ctrl\-C)\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take, for a \fB\-d\fR file that cannot be opened, and when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened\.
.P
A run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board exits 0 as well: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\.
.P
Also 1 when the dump's USB stream fails part\-way: the board stopped answering or dropped off the bus, so the dump stops there and holds only what arrived before it\. The failure is reported on standard error as it happens\. A board that never hands the transfer back at all exits 1 too, reported by the \fBwarning:\fR line ubertooth(7) describes rather than an error\.
.P
Also 1 when the board refuses the request that ends the dump: it is left in the capture mode, and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. What was dumped stays written out, and libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
Also 1 when the board refuses the modulation \fB\-c\fR or \fB\-l\fR asks for, with libubertooth's own line for the refused request on standard error and nothing of the dump on standard output: a dump that went ahead would hold whatever modulation the board had been left in, and nothing in its records says which that was\.
.P
The dump states it on either side of the request that starts its capture, for the reason ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS gives; that page also says what \fB\-l\fR can hold at the very start of a dump on a board another run left in a mode\. Nothing is asked of the radio until then, which is after a \fB\-d\fR file has been opened, so a refusal leaves that file there and empty, as a board that will not start the capture does, and a \fB\-d\fR the tool cannot open leaves the board as it was found\.
.P
A \fB\-d\fR file is opened once the board has been opened and its firmware accepted, not before, so a run refused for either reason leaves no empty file behind\. A terminal on standard output is still refused while the command line is read, with or without \fB\-d \-\fR, because that is a problem with the command rather than with the board\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
.SH "THE GENERIC RECEIVE"
Experimental: expect the options and the output to change\. Both halves of it work on our bench, the symbol stream and the packet framing\.
.P
With \fB\-g\fR the board does one of two things, and which one it does comes from GRMDM's packet\-mode bit, so the configuration decides it:
.IP "\(bu" 4
with packet mode clear, the board streams demodulated symbols as the Bluetooth captures do, so \fB\-b\fR prints them as \fB0\fR and \fB1\fR\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
with packet mode set, the board waits for the sync word in SYNCH and SYNCL and sends one record each time the radio reports one: the four sync\-word bytes, then a fixed 32 bytes read out of the radio's FIFO\. It reads no length and waits for no end of packet, so a record is a sample of the FIFO rather than necessarily one whole packet\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Packet mode leaves the framing to the radio, so it produces records only when GRMDM's preamble length, how much of the sync word it matches and its CRC choice all match what is on the air\. Getting that right for a protocol is part of the setup and nothing reports a mismatch: a wrong choice gives a capture with no records in it\. The one transmitter whose framing is known is \fBubertooth\-tx \-g\fR, so a second Ubertooth is the way to check a packet\-mode setup; ubertooth(7), GENERIC RADIO MODE has both ends of that\.
.P
Either way the records are the 68\-byte and 401\-byte shapes described above: the board sends what it received, and this tool writes it out without looking in it\. Nothing decodes a generic capture, because nothing here knows what protocol it is\. In a packet\-mode record the four sync\-word bytes are at offset 18 and the 32 payload bytes at offset 22, so \fBxxd \-s 22 \-l 32 packets\.bin\fR shows the first packet's payload\.
.P
The configuration has to be in the radio before the capture starts, and it survives only while the board is idle: a mode ending puts the radio back through its own reset\. So write the registers and start the dump as two commands with nothing in between, as ubertooth(7), GENERIC RADIO MODE shows\.
